After some scrutiny, I now suppose that you meant "It's ridiculous to
detach the individual uncertainty about each belief from the general
uncertainty about all beliefs". But this is still on the view that
certainty matters here. Leave all uncertain beliefs out of it, and the
paradox subsists.

Jorge says this is no paradox because it can be
resolved by saying where the quantifiers need to go. That assumes,
however, that it is legitimate to quantify into du'u clauses.
That gets you into trouble analogous to the other paradox
"Nine is necessarily greater than five, nine is the number of planets
in the solar system, so the number of planets in the solar system is
necessarily greater than five".

